# Messages of lib/checks/15-hardware.sh. Format and rules: see common.lang.

section.15-hardware=Hardware health

# ---- HW-BATTERY ----
HW-BATTERY.title.one={name} {pct} % of its original capacity
HW-BATTERY.title.unknown={name} (health unknown)
HW-BATTERY.info.title=Battery: {list}
HW-BATTERY.info.impact=A battery slowly loses capacity with age and charge cycles. Below {threshold} % of its original capacity, the audit reports it. Charge thresholds, when the computer offers them, slow down this wear: the battery stops charging before 100 %.
HW-BATTERY.warn.title=Worn battery: {list}
HW-BATTERY.warn.impact=The battery now holds less than {threshold} % of the charge it held when new: the computer runs much less time unplugged, and may shut down suddenly when the charge shown is still above zero. This is wear, not a software fault.
HW-BATTERY.warn.act.1=Shows the details of the battery as the system sees them (capacity, charge, state). Read-only.
HW-BATTERY.warn.act.2=If the shorter battery life bothers you, plan a replacement with a battery of the same model (manufacturer and model are in the evidence).
HW-BATTERY.warn.act.3=If the computer mostly stays plugged in, limit the charge to about 80 % in the firmware settings (BIOS/UEFI) or in the manufacturer's tool: this slows down further wear.
HW-BATTERY.warn.caution=A swollen battery (case that bulges, touchpad that lifts) is dangerous: stop using the computer on battery and have it replaced.
HW-BATTERY.ev.1=Batteries (from /sys/class/power_supply)
HW-BATTERY.ev.health=health {pct} % (full {full} / design {design} {unit})
HW-BATTERY.ev.health_unknown=health unknown (capacities not reported)
HW-BATTERY.ev.cycles={n} charge cycles
HW-BATTERY.ev.charge=charge {pct} %
HW-BATTERY.ev.status=state {status}
HW-BATTERY.ev.thresholds=charge thresholds {start}-{end} %
HW-BATTERY.ev.threshold_end=charge stops at {end} %
HW-BATTERY.ev.mode=charge mode {mode}

# ---- HW-DISK-HEALTH ----
HW-DISK-HEALTH.ok.title=Disk health (SMART) normal ({n} disk(s) read)
HW-DISK-HEALTH.warn.title=Disk worn out or reporting an alert: {disks}
HW-DISK-HEALTH.warn.impact=The disk itself reports a problem: its flash memory is nearly used up (90 % or more of its rated wear), or it raised an alert (spare blocks running out, temperature too high, backup power fault). It still works, but the risk of losing data grows.
HW-DISK-HEALTH.warn.act.1=Check that your important files are backed up, and that the backup is recent.
HW-DISK-HEALTH.warn.act.2=Shows the health of the disk and its counters (package smartmontools). Read-only.
HW-DISK-HEALTH.warn.act.3=Plan the replacement of the disk, then copy the system or your data onto the new one.
HW-DISK-HEALTH.warn.caution=A temperature alert can come from a blocked fan or a hot room: check this before concluding that the disk is failing.
HW-DISK-HEALTH.crit.title=Disk declaring itself failing: {disks}
HW-DISK-HEALTH.crit.impact=The disk's own self-test says it is failing (or, for an NVMe disk, that its reliability is degraded or that it switched to read-only). Data loss can happen at any moment, sometimes with no further warning.
HW-DISK-HEALTH.crit.act.1=Copy your important files to another disk or to a backup now, before anything else.
HW-DISK-HEALTH.crit.act.2=Shows the health of the disk and its counters (package smartmontools). Read-only.
HW-DISK-HEALTH.crit.act.3=Replace the disk as soon as possible; ask for help to copy the system onto the new one if needed.
HW-DISK-HEALTH.crit.caution=Until the data is copied, avoid heavy operations on this disk: file system repair, defragmentation, long self-tests or a large copy of everything at once. Copy the most important files first.
HW-DISK-HEALTH.ev.1=Disks examined (parsed values; serial numbers left out)
HW-DISK-HEALTH.ev.standby=asleep (not woken up: health not read)
HW-DISK-HEALTH.ev.nodata=no SMART data available
HW-DISK-HEALTH.ev.health=overall health {value}
HW-DISK-HEALTH.ev.cw_none=no critical warning
HW-DISK-HEALTH.ev.cw=critical warning: {flags}
HW-DISK-HEALTH.ev.wear=wear {pct} %
HW-DISK-HEALTH.ev.hours={n} h powered on
HW-DISK-HEALTH.ev.temp={c} °C
HW-DISK-HEALTH.ev.realloc={n} reallocated sectors
HW-DISK-HEALTH.ev.pending={n} pending sectors
HW-DISK-HEALTH.ev.media={n} media errors
HW-DISK-HEALTH.ev.source=source {src}
HW-DISK-HEALTH.partial.1=disk health (SMART) not read for {disks}: reading it needs the audit to run as root (sudo ./uhc.sh) with the package smartmontools installed, or the udisks2 service running
HW-DISK-HEALTH.partial.2=disk health (SMART) not read for {disks}: install the package smartmontools (it provides smartctl), then run the audit again

# ---- HW-KERNEL-ERRORS ----
HW-KERNEL-ERRORS.ok.title=No disk, file system or memory error in the kernel log (current and previous start-up)
HW-KERNEL-ERRORS.ok.title_current=No disk, file system or memory error in the kernel log (current start-up; the previous one is not kept)
HW-KERNEL-ERRORS.kind.io=disk read/write
HW-KERNEL-ERRORS.kind.fs=file system
HW-KERNEL-ERRORS.kind.hw=processor or memory
HW-KERNEL-ERRORS.warn.title=Hardware errors in the kernel log: {kinds}
HW-KERNEL-ERRORS.warn.impact=The kernel (the core of the system) recorded errors that usually come from the hardware: a disk that cannot read or write some data, a damaged file system, or a fault detected by the processor or the memory. A single error after unplugging a USB disk is harmless; repeated errors often announce a failure.
HW-KERNEL-ERRORS.warn.act.1=Shows the warnings and errors of the kernel since the computer started, with their time. Read-only.
HW-KERNEL-ERRORS.warn.act.2=Same for the previous start-up. Read-only.
HW-KERNEL-ERRORS.warn.act.3=If the errors name a disk, check that your important files are backed up, then look at the disk health result above.
HW-KERNEL-ERRORS.warn.caution=Do not repair a file system (fsck) while it is mounted, and copy your important files before any repair: a repair on a failing disk can make things worse.
HW-KERNEL-ERRORS.oom.title=Programs stopped for lack of memory: {n} time(s) since the previous start-up
HW-KERNEL-ERRORS.oom.impact=When the memory is full, the kernel stops a program to free some (the out-of-memory killer): unsaved work in that program is lost. This is not a hardware fault: memory is short for what runs on this computer.
HW-KERNEL-ERRORS.oom.act.1=Shows which programs were stopped and when. Read-only.
HW-KERNEL-ERRORS.ev.io=Disk read/write errors ({n} line(s); last ones of each start-up)
HW-KERNEL-ERRORS.ev.fs=File system errors ({n} line(s); last ones of each start-up)
HW-KERNEL-ERRORS.ev.hw=Processor or memory errors ({n} line(s); last ones of each start-up)
HW-KERNEL-ERRORS.ev.oom=Programs stopped for lack of memory ({n} line(s); last ones of each start-up)
HW-KERNEL-ERRORS.partial.1=hardware errors in the kernel log not checked: journalctl is not available
HW-KERNEL-ERRORS.partial.2=hardware errors in the kernel log not checked: the kernel log cannot be read with the current privileges (run as root, or as a member of the adm or systemd-journal group)
